---
import { instructionPlates, latencyBench } from '../data/site'

/**
 * Source builds remain a first-class distribution path, so these commands are
 * presented as real, selectable instructions in the monospace, on flat matte
 * plates.
 *
 * The latency strip is the page's one deliberate hole. No run of the bench
 * corpus exists, so instead of a plausible millisecond figure it names the
 * harness and the corpus and says so. A privacy tool that invents a number on
 * its own landing page has already told you how much its other numbers are
 * worth.
 */
---

<section class="band-top bg-night" aria-labelledby="operation-heading">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1536px] px-5 py-14 sm:px-8 lg:py-16">
    {
      /*
        No kicker. The one this band once carried, "No packages yet", described
        an earlier release state and made absence the product's emphasis.
      */
    }
    <h2 id="operation-heading" class="display-section text-ink">Build it and run it</h2>

    {
      /*
        Two columns at most, never four. At four the longest real command —
        `bun run tauri dev -- --features whisper-vulkan` — is clipped by its own
        plate at 1536px wide, and a build instruction the reader cannot finish
        reading is the worst thing this band could ship. The grid yields to the
        content's actual measure.
      */
    }
    <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        instructionPlates.map((p) => (
          <div class="plate flex flex-col">
            <h3 class="mono-micro text-ink-soft border-rule border-b px-5 py-3 uppercase">
              {p.legend}
            </h3>
            {/* One `pre` per plate, so a reader can select the whole block and
                paste it. Splitting each line into its own element would look
                identical and paste as garbage.

                `set:text` on a self-closing element rather than children,
                because whitespace inside a `<pre>` is content: with a nested
                `<code>` on its own line the formatter indents it, and every
                command shipped with fourteen leading spaces baked into the thing
                the reader is meant to copy. A childless element has no interior
                for a formatter to reflow. */}
            <pre
              class="mono-caption text-ink overflow-x-auto px-5 py-4"
              set:text={p.lines.join('\n')}
            />
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    {
      /*
        The hazard strip stays horizontal at every width. Rotated to a vertical
        6px edge the -45deg chevrons stop touching each other and read as a
        dashed line, which is a different sign entirely.
      */
    }
    {
      latencyBench.placeholder && (
        <div class="plate mt-6">
          <div class="chevron-strip h-1.5 w-full" aria-hidden="true" />
          <div class="px-5 py-4 md:py-5">
            {/* A plate heading, in the same off-white display label the model
                catalog and accelerator plates use, not a scarlet kicker: this
                titles a notice inside a plate rather than naming a band. The
                chevron edge above it is already the mark that says "read this
                one differently". */}
            <h3 class="display-label text-ink">Not yet measured</h3>
            <p class="mono-legend text-ink mt-2.5 max-w-[80ch]">
              There is no published latency figure, because no run of the bench corpus has been
              recorded. When there is one it will name the hardware it ran on.
            </p>
            <dl class="mono-caption text-ink-soft mt-3.5 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-1 sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr]">
              <dt class="uppercase">Harness</dt>
              <dd class="text-ink">
                <code>crates/shadoword-model-whisper/tests/whisper_integration.rs</code>
              </dd>
              <dt class="uppercase">Corpus</dt>
              <dd class="text-ink">
                <code>bench_corpus/clip_&#123;10,15,20,30&#125;s.wav</code>
              </dd>
              <dt class="uppercase">Model</dt>
              <dd class="text-ink">{latencyBench.model}</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      )
    }
  </div>
</section>
